Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.990 align:start position:0%
 
nuclear experts from the international  atomic energy are on their way to  ukraine's giant saparicia facility  they're aiming to check the safety of  the russian-occupied plant which has  seen several near disasters in recent  weeks  russia installed  russian authorities rather in the nearby  town of eden  accused ukraine of drone strikes on the  power station but russia is accusing  shelling by the ukrainians  and ukrainian-held nikopol on the  opposite bank of the nipa river and  other areas near the plant including the  city of safarico  today the head of the iaea mission bravo  grossi said they were going ahead  despite reports of continued shelling  we are moving  we are aware of the current situation  there has been  increased military activity including  this morning um until very recently a  few minutes ago i have been briefed by  the ukrainian regional military uh  commander  here about that and the inherent risks  but weighing the pros and cons  and having come so far we are not  stopping  we are moving now we know that there is  a  an area as you know the so-called gray  zone where the last line of the  ukrainian defense comes and before the  first line of the russian  occupying forces begin  where the risks are significant  at the same time  we consider that we  have the minimum conditions to move  accepting that the risks are very very  high  dw correspondent matthias berlinger is  standing by for us in safarisia matthias  what more do we know about the un team's  mission today  yeah the hope is to arrive at the plant  today and to start their work what  they're going to do there is to  see the working conditions of the people  who are working there whether they can  work in conditions that ensure safety  with all the safety protocols are still  in place the uh  safety mechanisms still working whether  the fuel that has been stored there is  still there and things like that it's a  dangerous trip he has just said it  because they have to cross the line it's  the first time that the iaea has to  cross uh a front line in a war um and  there have been has been shelling this  morning and there have been warnings  that this might not go ahead we've just  heard uh seen the head of the military  administration here saying that the  mission might not be able to proceed we  don't know much about  much more now  and there are reports that this shelling  continues with i guess both sides again  blaming each other  yes that's what's happening there was  shelling again in the at the site uh in  and there has also been shelling at  least that's what the ukrainian side  says off the road there the uh shelling  the ukrainians accused the russians off  um of the gray zone that he has just  been speaking of how successful  can this mission  really be considering they do need time  and time is not on their side  so the hope is that the some of the  inspectors that have that are going  there now will stay there and will  continuously monitor the situation the  russians have in principle said that  they would agree to that and uh the iaea  has said that they want to do that that  would of course be crucial because um  in a day or two uh it's possible to just  clean up a few things and you know  present a picture and then  what happens after they leave is unclear  so staying there continuously monitoring  would probably uh enhance the  effectivity of this mission by a lot  but that would take commitment from both  the russians and the ukrainians in in  trying to find a joint solution here  wouldn't it  i think the ball is on the russian side  if the refresher agrees that they can  stay there they can stay there ukraine  doesn't have any influence on what's  happening on site um i think the most  important part from the ukrainian side  was to allow safe passage for them to  negotiate the route of passage to ensure  that nothing will happen to them on the  way that was the ukrainian part they  also gave them information about what  they should inspect their technical  details about the power plant but  whether they can stay on site is more a  decision that the russians have to make  dw's matthias burlinger many thanks  let's get more from former british army  officer and military analyst mike martin  how likely is russia to allow for an  extended monitoring mission in south  asia  i think russia's playing games at the  moment so your listeners  viewers might recall that at the  beginning there was a big argument about  whether the team was going to come  through kiev um  through the ukrainian side whether it's  going to come through russia the  russians lost that battle so now what  they're doing is playing games and  saying well you know you can only come  for a day or you've got to queue up like  everyone else at the checkpoints we  can't give you uh extended passes so  really it's just in the russians hands  we've also been reporting on ukraine's  counter-offensive in hassan for days now  about whether or not it will go ahead  and in what form what more can you tell  us about what's going on  um we don't want to go into too many  details for obvious reasons and the  ukrainians are  operating a very strict media blackout  but the offensive has started  and the phase that they're in at the  moment is what's called a shaping phase  and and what you do in a shaping phase  is you uh you take out infrastructure so  the ukrainians have hit a couple of  bridges over the last 48 hours  you continue to cut target air defense  command and control and supply  there's there's probably about 10 or 20  000 russian soldiers  in that pocket north of the dinipro  river it will be impossible to kill  every last one of them so what the  ukrainians are trying to do is shape the  battlefield  to create situations where they collapse  or run away but we shouldn't expect this  to be quick it's 10 000 russian soldiers  um but we will see this play out over  the next month or so  at the same time i think it's important  to  bring up uh these reports that uh we're  hearing of u.s intelligence officials  saying that russia's military is facing  severe manpower shortages but at the  same time there have been reports that  the kremlin is also assembling a third  army corps what can what can you tell us  there  well i i think there's two things we  have to look at here one is quantity of  soldiers and one is quality and russia  has suffered from a lack of both  throughout the war and you know they've  taken fairly substantial casualties as  well so the third army corps  they tried to assemble fifteen thousand  soldiers and they've managed to get  about five thousand and if we look at  the quality a lot of these guys are uh  they've extended the age limit to sixty  um there are reports of them offering  prisoners early release to go into it so  there will be about 5 000 very poorly  trained some reports say they're getting  two weeks uh soldiers i think the  russians are intending to position them  in the north of the country  to create a kind of a diversion or a  demonstration because it will mean that  ukraine will have to tie down some units  up in the north of the country but  really i think what this is is a a bit  of a distraction you know a little  annoyance that ukraine has to deal with  but it's not substantially going to  affect what is going on in the south in  herson and in crimea a question of  quality no quantity as you heard it  there from military analyst mike martin  thank you very much  thank you  you

